  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The number of subscribers $S=S(t)$ to a website $t$ months after a marketing campaign can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, and $S(2)=15\\,000$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k\u2248$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$",
  "worked_solution": "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & \u22480.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k\u224811%,$ rounded to the nearest percent.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
